    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**New step class** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`:\n- A small, single-responsibility function that logs one audit line to the SLF4J logger named `AUDIT` (Logback default backend), containing the owner id, `customerCode` and `registrationDate`.\n\n**Pipeline wiring** \u2014 `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`:\n- Inserted an `audit` step between `save` and `respond` (`save \u2192 audit \u2192 respond`).\n\n## Why here\n\nThe audit must run *after* `save`, since the owner `id` is generated on persist, and *before* `respond`. Rather than folding logging into `SaveOwner` (persistence) or `RespondWithOwnerCreated` (HTTP response) \u2014 which would make either a catch-all \u2014 auditing is its own concern, so it's its own step, consistent with the project's one-class-per-step convention and naming. It reads the persisted entity via `@Val Owner` and emits:\n\n```\nAUDIT - Owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-21\n```\n\nThe acceptance test `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` passes, asserting the AUDIT line contains the id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.",
